(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','ADD/DROP DEADLINE — Students may change their schedules without a transcript note until Friday at 5:00 p.m. After the deadline, a late swap requires approval from both instructors and is recorded on the transcript as a course change.','NONE','["A course swap made after Friday needs both instructors'' approval.","The deadline applies only to first-year students.","Late changes leave no record at all.","Instructors cannot refuse a late swap."]','A',1,'Option A: after Friday, both instructors must approve and the change is recorded; the notice is not limited to any class year.',16),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the exchange and answer the question.','Registrar staff: Bring your student ID and the signed advising form to window 4. I cannot issue a registration code without the form, and window 4 shuts its screen at 4:30, fifteen minutes before the office closes.','NONE','["Codes arrive by mail the next week.","The office closes at 4:30.","She must bring a signed form to one window before 4:30.","She can register online instead."]','C',1,'Option C names the real requirement: the signed form at window 4 before 4:30. The office itself closes at 4:45, so the 4:30 detail belongs only to the window.',17),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the form instruction and answer the question.','FORM S-2 — Print your family name exactly as shown on your passport. Tick one box only: full-time or part-time. Undated forms are returned to the applicant instead of being processed.','NONE','["Any number of boxes may be ticked.","Names may be written in any order.","A form without a date will be sent back.","Undated forms are processed late."]','C',1,'Option C restates the rule: missing dates cause return, and only one box may be ticked.',18),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','REGISTRATION HOLD — A hold has been placed on your record because the health insurance waiver was not uploaded. Courses you already added will be cancelled at 5:00 p.m. Friday unless the waiver is received by Thursday noon.','NONE','["The hold will lift automatically.","Cancelled courses return on Monday.","The waiver may be handed in on Friday.","A missing insurance document threatens her enrolment."]','D',1,'Option D captures the cause and consequence: without the waiver by Thursday noon, enrolment is cancelled on Friday.',19),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','LANGUAGE LAB HOURS — Individual booths are open 8:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m. and may be booked one hour at a time. Conversation rooms on the east wall host guided practice only between 1:00 and 3:00 p.m.','NONE','["Guided conversation practice takes place in the early afternoon.","Booths close at 8:00 p.m.","The booths are bookable all night.","Guided sessions run every hour."]','A',1,'Option A matches the 1:00-3:00 p.m. guided window; booths shut at 10:00 p.m. and open at 8:00 a.m.',20),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','BOOTH RULES — One person per booth. Food and drink are banned; water in a sealed bottle is allowed. Leave headphones on the desk and erase your recordings before you go.','NONE','["Snacks are acceptable if eaten quietly.","Only bottled water with a closed lid may come in.","Recordings must be kept after the session.","Two people may share a booth."]','B',1,'Option B is the exact exception on the sign; everything else open or unpacked is banned, and recordings must be erased.',21),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Hana: The recording software saved my speaking task, but I cannot find the file anywhere. — Tutor: Lab files sync to your account automatically at the end of each session. If you logged in as a guest, nothing was kept.','NONE','["The software needs an update.","The tutor deleted the file.","She recorded without signing into her account.","The lab never saves speaking tasks."]','C',1,'Option C: the tutor explains that a guest log-in skips the account sync, so nothing was kept.',22),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','SELF-ACCESS CENTRE — Forty pronunciation headsets have been upgraded. Because printed manuals no longer match the controls, the lab website now carries a six-minute video tour of each new menu. Bring the video on your own phone; the lab does not lend tablets.','NONE','["She should watch the guide on her own device.","She may borrow a lab tablet.","She should read the printed manual.","She must ask staff to upgrade her headset."]','A',1,'Option A: the tour lives on the website and must be carried on a personal phone, since no tablets are lent.',23),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','CAMPUS SHUTTLE — Line 3 loops between the station, the library and the stadium every twelve minutes until 7:00 p.m. After 7:00 p.m. the loop becomes a demand service: request a stop in the app two stops ahead, and the bus skips stops nobody has asked for.','NONE','["After 7:00 p.m. the shuttle serves only requested stops.","Line 3 stops running at 7:00 p.m.","The loop gets faster after dark.","Requests must be made at the station."]','A',1,'Option A restates the demand-service rule: the shuttle still runs after 7:00 p.m. but skips unrequested stops.',24),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the text alert and answer the question.','SHUTTLE ALERT — Roadwork closes the library drop-off bay all day today. Line 1 will board at the north plaza instead, a four-minute walk along the covered path. Normal stops resume at 9:00 a.m. tomorrow.','NONE','["Buses are cancelled for the whole day.","Passengers should board from the north plaza today.","The roadwork will last a week.","The covered path is closed."]','B',1,'Option B: only the boarding point changes today, via the covered path, with normal service resuming tomorrow morning.',25),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the timetable note and answer the question.','TIMETABLE CORRECTION — The printed card shows a 6:45 a.m. stadium run; that trip exists only on match days. The permanent first departure is 7:30 a.m. from the station, and the last return leaves the stadium at 11:15 p.m.','NONE','["The 6:45 trip runs every morning.","The last return bus leaves at midnight.","The earliest daily departure is 7:30 a.m.","No shuttle leaves the station before 8:00 a.m."]','C',1,'Option C follows the correction: 6:45 is match-day only, so the daily first bus is 7:30; the final return is 11:15 p.m.',26),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the shelter sign and answer the question.','SHELTER 7 — Real-time arrival displays work only when your card is scanned. Paper passes are valid on every route, but the driver cannot check them aloud; passengers holding one must track the next stops themselves.','NONE','["Paper passes are refused at this shelter.","Scanning a card costs one fare unit.","The display works even without a card.","A paper-pass rider must follow the stops on his or her own."]','D',1,'Option D: paper passes ride fine, but without a scanned card the live display stays blank, so the rider counts stops alone.',27),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','MUSEUM TOURS — The free one-hour highlights tour starts at 11:00 a.m. from the front desk on Saturdays, and the portrait collection runs its own guided walk at 2:00 p.m. every day. Galleries close fifteen minutes before the building.','NONE','["The portrait tour is available daily.","The highlights tour charges a fee.","Galleries close at the building''s closing hour.","Both tours start at noon."]','A',1,'Option A: the portrait walk happens every day at 2:00 p.m.; only the highlights tour is Saturday-only, and galleries shut early.',28),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Visitor: Do we need a reservation for the rooftop sculpture walk? — Guide: Seats are capped at twenty, and all of them went when bookings opened at noon yesterday. The ground-floor courtyard viewing is open to anyone without a ticket.','NONE','["She should book at noon today.","She can join the courtyard viewing without a booking.","The rooftop walk is now free.","The walk lasts an hour."]','B',1,'Option B: the rooftop is sold out, but the courtyard needs no ticket; bookings had opened the day before.',29),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','EDUCATION DEPARTMENT — Your school booking for March 3 is confirmed. Our staff noticed your list requests a working-artist demonstration, which runs only on the first Saturday of each month; a filmed studio visit will be shown instead, and the live demonstration can be added on a later trip.','NONE','["The trip has been postponed.","The demonstration will run as planned.","One requested activity will be replaced.","The school must rebook."]','C',1,'Option C: March 3 is not a first Saturday, so the film substitutes for the live demonstration while the booking itself stands.',30),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','AUDIO GUIDES — Insert coins or tap your transit card, then press the green button for your language. Headsets already issued are programmed for floor two; ask the front desk to reset one before you go upstairs.','NONE','["Transit cards are refused.","The green button cancels the guide.","Only one language is offered.","A headset may need resetting for another floor."]','D',1,'Option D: issued headsets default to floor two, so a visitor heading upstairs should have the desk reset the device.',31),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','STUDENT HEALTH — Same-day sick appointments are given until 11:30 a.m. every weekday, in person only. Online booking opens two weeks ahead and slots go quickly; a certificate for missed class costs nothing but must be collected before 4:00 p.m.','NONE','["Walking in before noon is the quickest way to see a doctor.","Certificates carry a small fee.","Online slots appear the same morning.","Certificates may be collected after 4:00 p.m."]','A',1,'Option A: the walk-in window closes at 11:30 a.m., so early arrival beats waiting on a fast-selling online slot.',32),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','NURSE EMAIL — Your blood test shows a vitamin deficiency. The health shop stocks the supplement we recommend; take it with food, and a follow-up blood draw is set for six weeks from today. Nothing further is needed unless symptoms worsen.','NONE','["She must change her diet completely.","She can buy the supplement inside the campus service.","A hospital visit is required this week.","The blood test must be repeated tomorrow."]','B',1,'Option B: the recommended supplement is available at the campus health shop, with the recheck scheduled six weeks out.',33),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Sam: I sprained my wrist at badminton and cannot type my essay. — Coordinator: The health centre can write a two-day extension note. Send the request from the student portal tonight, and pick up the signed note at the front desk tomorrow morning.','NONE','["The essay deadline has been cancelled.","The note will arrive by mail.","An extension request goes through the portal.","Badminton is suspended this week."]','C',1,'Option C: the coordinator directs Sam to file the request on the portal tonight before collecting the note.',34),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','STUDY-ABROAD FAIR — Forty partner universities will staff tables from 1:00 to 4:30 p.m. in the sports hall. Bring printed transcripts if you want a pre-evaluation at a table; several officers will email their own checklists to students the same evening.','NONE','["The fair runs the whole week.","Checklists are available only by post.","Entry requires registration weeks ahead.","A table pre-evaluation needs paper records."]','D',1,'Option D: the flyer ties pre-evaluation to printed transcripts; checklists come by email that evening from some officers.',35),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly explain?','Down where sunlight cannot reach, roughly three quarters of the animals produce their own light. Marine organisms generate this glow through a chemical reaction in which a molecule called luciferin is oxidised by an enzyme, luciferase, releasing energy as blue-green photons that travel far through seawater. Creatures use the light for different ends. Some hatchetfish line their bellies with light organs that match the faint glow from above, erasing their outline from predators looking upward. Anglerfish dangle a luminous lure to draw prey close enough to swallow. Others release bright clouds of glowing fluid to startle an attacker and escape in the confusion. Because blue and green wavelengths cross the greatest distance in deep water, almost every measured species emits within that narrow band, whatever chemistry it uses. Researchers now harvest the same proteins to tag genes in laboratories, so a trait that began as a way to survive darkness has become one of biology''s most widely used tools.','NONE','["What deep-sea creatures use self-made light for and how scientists have borrowed the same chemistry","Why blue light travels farther than other colours in seawater","How hatchetfish escape from anglerfish","Which chemicals are needed to build luciferin"]','A',1,'Option A covers the full arc: the passage lists the uses of the glow and ends with laboratory applications of the same proteins.',36),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "harvest" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Down where sunlight cannot reach, roughly three quarters of the animals produce their own light. Marine organisms generate this glow through a chemical reaction in which a molecule called luciferin is oxidised by an enzyme, luciferase, releasing energy as blue-green photons that travel far through seawater. Creatures use the light for different ends. Some hatchetfish line their bellies with light organs that match the faint glow from above, erasing their outline from predators looking upward. Anglerfish dangle a luminous lure to draw prey close enough to swallow. Others release bright clouds of glowing fluid to startle an attacker and escape in the confusion. Because blue and green wavelengths cross the greatest distance in deep water, almost every measured species emits within that narrow band, whatever chemistry it uses. Researchers now harvest the same proteins to tag genes in laboratories, so a trait that began as a way to survive darkness has become one of biology''s most widely used tools.','NONE','["grow","collect","measure","destroy"]','B',1,'Option B: researchers obtain and purify the proteins for lab use, so "harvest" = collect.',37),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about predators in the deep sea?','Down where sunlight cannot reach, roughly three quarters of the animals produce their own light. Marine organisms generate this glow through a chemical reaction in which a molecule called luciferin is oxidised by an enzyme, luciferase, releasing energy as blue-green photons that travel far through seawater. Creatures use the light for different ends. Some hatchetfish line their bellies with light organs that match the faint glow from above, erasing their outline from predators looking upward. Anglerfish dangle a luminous lure to draw prey close enough to swallow. Others release bright clouds of glowing fluid to startle an attacker and escape in the confusion. Because blue and green wavelengths cross the greatest distance in deep water, almost every measured species emits within that narrow band, whatever chemistry it uses. Researchers now harvest the same proteins to tag genes in laboratories, so a trait that began as a way to survive darkness has become one of biology''s most widely used tools.','NONE','["Most deep-sea animals are too small to glow.","Glow colour varies widely among species.","Many hunters in the deep attack from below.","Luminous fluid is the main food source."]','C',1,'Option C follows from the hatchetfish: it hides its outline from eyes looking upward, which implies attackers commonly hunt from beneath.',38),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why were homing pigeons valued by armies before radio?','Before radio nets and telephone cables, an army in the field could send a message no faster than a rider on horseback, and homing pigeons offered a route that no ambush could cut. A pigeon released far from its home loft flies back by combining cues: the position of the sun, sensitivity to the earth''s magnetic field, and landmarks memorised on earlier training flights. Handlers fitted racing baskets to trucks, trains and even messenger dogs, moving birds close to the front so the return journey stayed short. During the First World War, signal battalions carried up to twelve birds in sealed boxes; a clipped capsule on one leg held a text strip under two hundred words. The bird Cher Ami is credited with saving a surrounded battalion in 1918 by delivering a plea despite a wound. Pigeon services ended in most armies during the late twentieth century, replaced by radios small enough for one soldier to carry.','NONE','["They flew faster than horses over long distances.","They needed no training before service.","They could read the short texts attached to them","They carried messages along a path no ambush could cut."]','D',1,'Option D quotes the passage''s claim: unlike wires or riders, the bird''s homing route could not be intercepted along the way.',39),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "credited" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Before radio nets and telephone cables, an army in the field could send a message no faster than a rider on horseback, and homing pigeons offered a route that no ambush could cut. A pigeon released far from its home loft flies back by combining cues: the position of the sun, sensitivity to the earth''s magnetic field, and landmarks memorised on earlier training flights. Handlers fitted racing baskets to trucks, trains and even messenger dogs, moving birds close to the front so the return journey stayed short. During the First World War, signal battalions carried up to twelve birds in sealed boxes; a clipped capsule on one leg held a text strip under two hundred words. The bird Cher Ami is credited with saving a surrounded battalion in 1918 by delivering a plea despite a wound. Pigeon services ended in most armies during the late twentieth century, replaced by radios small enough for one soldier to carry.','NONE','["recognised","rewarded","invented","described"]','A',1,'Option A: people acknowledge Cher Ami with the rescue, so "credited" = recognised.',40),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about moving pigeon baskets close to the front?','Before radio nets and telephone cables, an army in the field could send a message no faster than a rider on horseback, and homing pigeons offered a route that no ambush could cut. A pigeon released far from its home loft flies back by combining cues: the position of the sun, sensitivity to the earth''s magnetic field, and landmarks memorised on earlier training flights. Handlers fitted racing baskets to trucks, trains and even messenger dogs, moving birds close to the front so the return journey stayed short. During the First World War, signal battalions carried up to twelve birds in sealed boxes; a clipped capsule on one leg held a text strip under two hundred words. The bird Cher Ami is credited with saving a surrounded battalion in 1918 by delivering a plea despite a wound. Pigeon services ended in most armies during the late twentieth century, replaced by radios small enough for one soldier to carry.','NONE','["It let birds fly home instead of being trained outward","It shortened the flight over which the message would arrive","It confused the bird''s magnetic cues","It was forbidden after 1918"]','B',1,'Option B: pigeons only ever fly back to the loft, so releasing them nearer the front cuts the distance the message must travel.',41),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly explain?','City towers of eight storeys and above have long been built from concrete and steel, and the reasons seemed fixed: strength, fire rules, cost. Now wooden high-rises are appearing again because engineers learned to laminate thin boards of spruce or fir into huge panels, cross-laminated timber, whose layers run at right angles and give the sheet a stiffness once demanded of a concrete slab. Wood stores the carbon its tree absorbed, while cement production releases much carbon dioxide, so a timber frame can cut a building''s embedded emissions by a third or more. Construction sites improve too: panels arrive cut to shape, and crews bolt them together without the waiting days that concrete requires to set, shortening schedules by a quarter in recent projects. Fire is managed by sizing timbers generously, since a charred outer layer insulates the core. Cost remains the open question; in several cities developers report that the panels themselves are dearer than the concrete they replace.','NONE','["Why concrete will soon vanish from skylines","How to laminate boards of spruce and fir","New engineered wood panels and carbon goals are bringing wooden towers back to cities","Why fires spread quickly through timber frames"]','C',1,'Option C frames the whole passage: the return of wooden high-rises is explained by cross-laminated panels and emissions savings.',42),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "set" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','City towers of eight storeys and above have long been built from concrete and steel, and the reasons seemed fixed: strength, fire rules, cost. Now wooden high-rises are appearing again because engineers learned to laminate thin boards of spruce or fir into huge panels, cross-laminated timber, whose layers run at right angles and give the sheet a stiffness once demanded of a concrete slab. Wood stores the carbon its tree absorbed, while cement production releases much carbon dioxide, so a timber frame can cut a building''s embedded emissions by a third or more. Construction sites improve too: panels arrive cut to shape, and crews bolt them together without the waiting days that concrete requires to set, shortening schedules by a quarter in recent projects. Fire is managed by sizing timbers generously, since a charred outer layer insulates the core. Cost remains the open question; in several cities developers report that the panels themselves are dearer than the concrete they replace.','NONE','["soften","break","cool","harden"]','D',1,'Option D: poured concrete must harden before the next stage, which is the delay timber avoids; "set" = harden.',43),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, how do timber towers save money?','City towers of eight storeys and above have long been built from concrete and steel, and the reasons seemed fixed: strength, fire rules, cost. Now wooden high-rises are appearing again because engineers learned to laminate thin boards of spruce or fir into huge panels, cross-laminated timber, whose layers run at right angles and give the sheet a stiffness once demanded of a concrete slab. Wood stores the carbon its tree absorbed, while cement production releases much carbon dioxide, so a timber frame can cut a building''s embedded emissions by a third or more. Construction sites improve too: panels arrive cut to shape, and crews bolt them together without the waiting days that concrete requires to set, shortening schedules by a quarter in recent projects. Fire is managed by sizing timbers generously, since a charred outer layer insulates the core. Cost remains the open question; in several cities developers report that the panels themselves are dearer than the concrete they replace.','NONE','["Mainly by finishing the build sooner","By using cheaper raw timber","By skipping fire inspections","By needing fewer bolts than steel"]','A',1,'Option A fits the last two sentences: panels cost more than concrete, so savings come chiefly from the quarter-shorter schedule.',44),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, how do flat platforms protect mountain fields?','Cut into a dry mountainside, a terrace is a staircase of flat fields held behind low walls, and each step is an engineered answer to shortage. On a steep slope, rain that arrives in minutes runs downhill, taking soil with it and leaving roots dry; the level platforms slow water until it sinks where the crops stand. Farmers in the Andes, the Philippines and the Atlas Mountains built walls of fitted stone that hold soil but drain freely, so a wet season does not drown the field. Water from a spring or glacier stream enters an open channel along the terrace''s upper edge, and small gates or plugged stones pass measured amounts from one step to the next, stretching a single flow across hundreds of plots. Because the walls absorb daytime heat and release it after dusk, the platforms soften frost, one reason terraced valleys ripen potatoes and maize that fail on the bare ridge above. Maintenance is continuous and communal: a season without cleared channels is a season of collapses.','NONE','["They keep sunlight away from the roots","They hold water long enough for it to soak in instead of washing soil away","They raise the air temperature permanently","They stop crops from ripening early"]','B',1,'Option B restates the core mechanism: level ground slows rain so it infiltrates rather than eroding the slope.',45),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "shortage" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Cut into a dry mountainside, a terrace is a staircase of flat fields held behind low walls, and each step is an engineered answer to shortage. On a steep slope, rain that arrives in minutes runs downhill, taking soil with it and leaving roots dry; the level platforms slow water until it sinks where the crops stand. Farmers in the Andes, the Philippines and the Atlas Mountains built walls of fitted stone that hold soil but drain freely, so a wet season does not drown the field. Water from a spring or glacier stream enters an open channel along the terrace''s upper edge, and small gates or plugged stones pass measured amounts from one step to the next, stretching a single flow across hundreds of plots. Because the walls absorb daytime heat and release it after dusk, the platforms soften frost, one reason terraced valleys ripen potatoes and maize that fail on the bare ridge above. Maintenance is continuous and communal: a season without cleared channels is a season of collapses.','NONE','["danger","drainage","lack","erosion"]','C',1,'Option C: terraces answer a scarcity of water in an arid mountain, so "shortage" = lack.',46),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about a terrace system whose upkeep stops?','Cut into a dry mountainside, a terrace is a staircase of flat fields held behind low walls, and each step is an engineered answer to shortage. On a steep slope, rain that arrives in minutes runs downhill, taking soil with it and leaving roots dry; the level platforms slow water until it sinks where the crops stand. Farmers in the Andes, the Philippines and the Atlas Mountains built walls of fitted stone that hold soil but drain freely, so a wet season does not drown the field. Water from a spring or glacier stream enters an open channel along the terrace''s upper edge, and small gates or plugged stones pass measured amounts from one step to the next, stretching a single flow across hundreds of plots. Because the walls absorb daytime heat and release it after dusk, the platforms soften frost, one reason terraced valleys ripen potatoes and maize that fail on the bare ridge above. Maintenance is continuous and communal: a season without cleared channels is a season of collapses.','NONE','["It would gain extra planting space","Walls would generate more heat","The fields would need new crops each season","Uncleared channels could bring the system down within a season"]','D',1,'Option D draws on the closing warning: one neglected season equals collapses, so the whole structure fails quickly without communal upkeep.',47),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the main idea of the passage?','Students who cram late into the night often feel more prepared and remember less, and experiments suggest the missing ingredient is sleep itself. During deep, slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus replays the day''s activity in compressed bursts, and each replay strengthens links between nerve cells while moving information toward longer storage in the cortex. REM sleep, marked by rapid eye movements and vivid dreaming, appears to sort these traces, keeping connections useful for the future and weakening ones that merely record trivia. Remove one stage and the other cannot compensate: learners deprived of slow-wave sleep show little gain the next morning even when total hours in bed are unchanged. Practice mixed with sleep also beats extra practice without it, a result found in music, language and motor tasks alike. The practical rule researchers offer is blunt: an hour taken from sleep and given to rereading is usually a poor exchange, because the rehearsal the brain performs after dark cannot be scheduled twice.','NONE','["Sleep replays and reorganises the day''s memories in ways extra study cannot replace","Dreams reveal wishes the waking mind hides","Cramming reliably improves test scores","REM sleep lasts longer than slow-wave sleep"]','A',1,'Option A states the passage''s claim: both sleep stages actively rebuild memory, and trading sleep for more reading loses.',48),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "deprived" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Students who cram late into the night often feel more prepared and remember less, and experiments suggest the missing ingredient is sleep itself. During deep, slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus replays the day''s activity in compressed bursts, and each replay strengthens links between nerve cells while moving information toward longer storage in the cortex. REM sleep, marked by rapid eye movements and vivid dreaming, appears to sort these traces, keeping connections useful for the future and weakening ones that merely record trivia. Remove one stage and the other cannot compensate: learners deprived of slow-wave sleep show little gain the next morning even when total hours in bed are unchanged. Practice mixed with sleep also beats extra practice without it, a result found in music, language and motor tasks alike. The practical rule researchers offer is blunt: an hour taken from sleep and given to rereading is usually a poor exchange, because the rehearsal the brain performs after dark cannot be scheduled twice.','NONE','["relieved","denied","reminded","cured"]','B',1,'Option B: learners prevented from getting slow-wave sleep gain nothing, so "deprived" = denied.',49),
(@set04_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred from the music and language results?','Students who cram late into the night often feel more prepared and remember less, and experiments suggest the missing ingredient is sleep itself. During deep, slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus replays the day''s activity in compressed bursts, and each replay strengthens links between nerve cells while moving information toward longer storage in the cortex. REM sleep, marked by rapid eye movements and vivid dreaming, appears to sort these traces, keeping connections useful for the future and weakening ones that merely record trivia. Remove one stage and the other cannot compensate: learners deprived of slow-wave sleep show little gain the next morning even when total hours in bed are unchanged. Practice mixed with sleep also beats extra practice without it, a result found in music, language and motor tasks alike. The practical rule researchers offer is blunt: an hour taken from sleep and given to rereading is usually a poor exchange, because the rehearsal the brain performs after dark cannot be scheduled twice.','NONE','["Motor skills never improve overnight","Sleep after practice helps more than the same time spent practising without sleep","Only language memory depends on dreaming","REM sleep erases everything learned that day"]','C',1,'Option C generalises the comparison: practice plus sleep outperformed additional practice alone across music, language and movement tasks.',50);
