(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you show me where the add-drop form is filed?','TTS','["Once a week.","Third drawer, right of the printer.","Yes, it is due Friday.","The form took an hour."]','B',1,'Option B gives a location, which is what the request needs; the other replies answer time or yes/no questions.',51),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Whose booth reservation is still on for this afternoon?','TTS','["The lab opens at eight.","Two hours at most.","It was Hana''s, but she gave it up.","Yes, I reserved it."]','C',1,'Option C names the person whose booking it was, answering the whose-question directly.',52),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you want the shuttle from the station or from the plaza?','TTS','["Every fifteen minutes.","It left already on Line 3.","Yes, the bus comes.","Neither, I am walking today."]','D',1,'Option D picks one side of the or-question by rejecting both, the natural reply; A and C answer other questions.',53),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Can I borrow your student card to scan at the shelter?','TTS','["Sure, here it is.","Yes, at the shelter.","It scans pretty quickly.","I ride every day."]','A',1,'Option A is a direct acceptance of a request to borrow something; the rest dodge the request.',54),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How much does the guided tour cost for a student?','TTS','["About two hours.","Nothing, as long as you show an ID.","On the first floor next to the desk.","It starts at noon."]','B',1,'Option B answers the price question; hours, place and start time are the wrong question types.',55),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Have you finished the audio guide on this floor yet?','TTS','["By the front desk.","It lasts about an hour.","Almost, one room left.","Green, I think."]','C',1,'Option C reports completion status, which the yet-question asks for; the others give place, length or a colour.',56),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you mind holding my notes while I tap my card?','TTS','["It costs two coins.","The reader beeps twice.","I have used this card before.","Not at all, go ahead."]','D',1,'Option D consents to the favour requested, the expected reply to a do-you-mind question.',57),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','What time does the same-day clinic desk open?','TTS','["Right at eight in the morning.","In the sports hall.","About ten patients an hour.","It opened two weeks ago."]','A',1,'Option A answers the what-time question; the others give place, volume or history.',58),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Is the health shop on this floor or upstairs?','TTS','["Every weekday of the term.","It is just past the nurses'' office.","The clinic opened at eight.","Once a month."]','B',1,'Option B states the location and resolves the either/or; A and D answer frequency, C a time.',59),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Did anyone pick up my extension note from the front desk?','TTS','["It costs nothing at all.","A brand-new certificate.","Yes, she is the nurse.","I have it, she asked me to carry it."]','D',1,'Option D answers the yes/no question and identifies who holds the note.',60),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Which table at the study-abroad fair did you visit?','TTS','["Nearly forty universities.","Somewhere in the sports hall.","Because of the deadline.","The one for the northern universities."]','D',1,'Option D names a specific table, answering which; the others give quantity, place or reason.',61),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you bring your printed transcript to the advising table tomorrow?','TTS','["Yes, I will print it tonight.","It is nearly forty pages.","The fair ends at 4:30.","She is an adviser, I think."]','A',1,'Option A accepts the request and fits the timeline; the others misread the question.',62),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Why did the registration system reject your form?','TTS','["It works every day except Sunday.","Because I forgot to tick the status box.","By noon, I guess.","Yes, it rejects incomplete forms."]','B',1,'Option B supplies a reason for the rejection, matching why; A and C give time, D is a yes/no answer.',63),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you know how long the roadwork on library lane will last?','TTS','["Yes, it lasts very long.","The shuttle was late again.","Just today, according to the alert.","Nearly forty minutes ago."]','C',1,'Option C answers the duration of the roadwork with the detail from the alert.',64),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Shall we book the rooftop sculpture walk for next week?','TTS','["The view from the roof is lovely.","Twenty people only, they say.","Bookings open at noon online.","It is already full, so try the courtyard instead."]','D',1,'Option D responds to the suggestion with a decision and alternative; the others only recite facts.',65),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Where is the nurse''s office on this floor?','TTS','["Second door after the lift.","She is here from eight.","An appointment about an hour ago.","Yes, here is my card."]','A',1,'Option A gives the location asked for; B answers when, C when, D responds to a request for ID.',66),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Did the tutor explain how the new headsets work?','TTS','["By the east wall of the lab.","Yes, in a short video on the site.","Forty of them arrived.","They look brand new."]','B',1,'Option B confirms the explanation happened and says where; the others answer quantity, place or looks.',67),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Would a 9:00 booth suit you for our practice swap?','TTS','["Two booths and a room.","Yes, it suits at nine.","9:30 would work better for me.","The booth is free right now."]','C',1,'Option C accepts the exchange idea but counter-proposes a time, the natural answer to a would-it-suit question.',68),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I registered for five courses yesterday, but one vanished from my schedule this morning. — Clerk: That happens when an advising hold is added overnight; nothing you chose is lost, it simply moves to pending until a counsellor signs. Come back tomorrow with your form.','TTS','["Another student took the seat.","Her password stopped working.","The office deleted the class on purpose.","The course should return once an overnight hold is cleared."]','D',1,'Option D matches the clerk: the course is pending, not lost, until the hold is resolved.',69),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I registered for five courses yesterday, but one vanished from my schedule this morning. — Clerk: That happens when an advising hold is added overnight; nothing you chose is lost, it simply moves to pending until a counsellor signs. Come back tomorrow with your form.','TTS','["See a counsellor and return the next day.","Pay the registration fee again.","Choose a different course.","Register at another window."]','A',1,'Option A paraphrases the clerk''s instruction: a counsellor signature clears the hold, then the student returns.',70),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I registered for five courses yesterday, but one vanished from my schedule this morning. — Clerk: That happens when an advising hold is added overnight; nothing you chose is lost, it simply moves to pending until a counsellor signs. Come back tomorrow with your form.','TTS','["The student''s schedule will not be settled that afternoon.","The hold will be removed automatically.","Counsellors refuse to sign forms.","The system erases all pending courses nightly."]','A',1,'Option A infers the timetable logic: the fix requires a counsellor and a return visit tomorrow, so the schedule stays incomplete this afternoon.',71),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: The conversation room only let me book fifteen minutes, and my practice group needs an hour. — Tutor: Groups over four people get the studio instead, but you must send the roster to the lab mailbox first so we can unlock the door code. The mailbox feed updates codes overnight.','TTS','["The conversation room is out of order.","The door code never changes.","The group is too large for the small room.","Four people may not use the studio."]','C',1,'Option C: a group of five or more cannot take the four-person conversation room, so the tutor points them to the studio.',72),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: The conversation room only let me book fifteen minutes, and my practice group needs an hour. — Tutor: Groups over four people get the studio instead, but you must send the roster to the lab mailbox first so we can unlock the door code. The mailbox feed updates codes overnight.','TTS','["The roster email is what lets staff generate the door code.","The group must apply in person at the desk.","The studio opens only before noon.","Students may not share codes."]','A',1,'Option A states the causal chain the tutor gives: roster first, then the code is unlocked, updated nightly.',73),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: The conversation room only let me book fifteen minutes, and my practice group needs an hour. — Tutor: Groups over four people get the studio instead, but you must send the roster to the lab mailbox first so we can unlock the door code. The mailbox feed updates codes overnight.','TTS','["Booking a full hour for five people takes one extra step.","The lab forbids group practice entirely.","Every room has a fifteen-minute cap.","The tutor will send the roster for the student."]','B',1,'Option B draws the inference: the hour itself is possible in the studio once the roster step is done.',74),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: I texted the driver from the shelter, but the bus drove straight past. — Man: After seven, the request has to reach the app at least two stops before; a plain text message may not arrive in time. Use the app next trip and the driver gets an alert.','TTS','["The stop closed at seven.","The bus was already full.","Her request did not reach the driver in time.","Her transit card had expired."]','C',1,'Option C: the man explains that a plain text may not arrive in time, which is why the bus passed.',75),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: I texted the driver from the shelter, but the bus drove straight past. — Man: After seven, the request has to reach the app at least two stops before; a plain text message may not arrive in time. Use the app next trip and the driver gets an alert.','TTS','["Wait at the station for the first loop.","Call the office before boarding.","Board without scanning.","Send the request through the app instead of a text."]','D',1,'Option D is the man''s explicit advice for the next trip.',76),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: I texted the driver from the shelter, but the bus drove straight past. — Man: After seven, the request has to reach the app at least two stops before; a plain text message may not arrive in time. Use the app next trip and the driver gets an alert.','TTS','["A shelter with no app requests may stay unserved after seven.","Drivers read every text message at each stop.","The night line stops running entirely at seven.","Using the app costs an extra fare."]','A',1,'Option A infers the rule: after 7:00 the loop serves only stops requested through the app in time.',77),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Visitor: Our guide said the portrait walk starts at two, and it is already ten past. — Guide: The group left from the front desk, but it pauses fifteen minutes at the courtyard so latecomers can join; after that stop the walk moves on without pausing again. — Visitor: Then we should hurry to the courtyard.','TTS','["At the front desk where the walk began","At the courtyard, for a short window","Nowhere, the tour has finished","On the rooftop sculpture deck"]','B',1,'Option B: the fifteen-minute courtyard pause is the last chance to join.',78),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Visitor: Our guide said the portrait walk starts at two, and it is already ten past. — Guide: The group left from the front desk, but it pauses fifteen minutes at the courtyard so latecomers can join; after that stop the walk moves on without pausing again. — Visitor: Then we should hurry to the courtyard.','TTS','["The walk ends in the courtyard.","It repeats every hour all afternoon","The group makes no further stops after the courtyard.","Visitors without tickets cannot follow it"]','C',1,'Option C restates the guide''s warning that the walk does not pause again.',79),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Visitor: Our guide said the portrait walk starts at two, and it is already ten past. — Guide: The group left from the front desk, but it pauses fifteen minutes at the courtyard so latecomers can join; after that stop the walk moves on without pausing again. — Visitor: Then we should hurry to the courtyard.','TTS','["Ten minutes","Fifteen minutes","Half an hour","Twenty minutes"]','B',1,'Option B quotes the pause length stated by the guide.',80),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention students. The health centre will move to the temporary clinic in the sports hall next Monday and stay there until the plumbing work is finished, a period staff expect to last three weeks. Appointment times will not change, but the pharmacy window at the new site serves morning patients only, and prescriptions collected there must be signed for in person.','TTS','["New opening hours at the health centre","A short-term relocation of one campus service","A free vaccination day","A lost prescription"]','B',1,'Option B: the notice is about the three-week move of the clinic to the sports hall.',81),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention students. The health centre will move to the temporary clinic in the sports hall next Monday and stay there until the plumbing work is finished, a period staff expect to last three weeks. Appointment times will not change, but the pharmacy window at the new site serves morning patients only, and prescriptions collected there must be signed for in person.','TTS','["They are cancelled for three weeks","They shift to evenings","They keep the same times at a different location","They must be booked a month ahead"]','C',1,'Option C restates the announcement: appointment times do not change despite the move.',82),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention students. The health centre will move to the temporary clinic in the sports hall next Monday and stay there until the plumbing work is finished, a period staff expect to last three weeks. Appointment times will not change, but the pharmacy window at the new site serves morning patients only, and prescriptions collected there must be signed for in person.','TTS','["An afternoon patient may have to collect the next morning or elsewhere","Machines will replace the window","Prescriptions become free during the move","Signatures are no longer needed"]','A',1,'Option A follows from the limits: the window works mornings only and needs an in-person signature, so afternoon pick-up fails.',83),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','A reminder about the study-abroad fair. This year the event spans two evenings, and the second session adds visa officers, a scholarship workshop every half hour, and a quiet room for recorded interviews. Doors at the sports hall open at noon on both days. Students who pre-registered receive a printed map at the entrance; everyone else gets a digital version by email.','TTS','["The fair now charges admission","Visa officers and recurring short workshops","The event is held off campus","Printed maps are withdrawn"]','B',1,'Option B lists exactly what the second session adds, according to the announcement.',84),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','A reminder about the study-abroad fair. This year the event spans two evenings, and the second session adds visa officers, a scholarship workshop every half hour, and a quiet room for recorded interviews. Doors at the sports hall open at noon on both days. Students who pre-registered receive a printed map at the entrance; everyone else gets a digital version by email.','TTS','["At nine in the morning","Only on the second evening","At midday on both days","Half an hour before each workshop"]','C',1,'Option C quotes the opening time: doors open at noon on both days.',85),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','A reminder about the study-abroad fair. This year the event spans two evenings, and the second session adds visa officers, a scholarship workshop every half hour, and a quiet room for recorded interviews. Doors at the sports hall open at noon on both days. Students who pre-registered receive a printed map at the entrance; everyone else gets a digital version by email.','TTS','["No entry without a booking","A printed map at the desk","A pass for the quiet room","A digital map sent by email"]','D',1,'Option D: visitors who did not pre-register receive the map electronically rather than on paper.',86),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Transit notice. Starting today, the night shuttle replaces the library lane stops with a pick-up point at the north plaza because of roadwork, and the last departure from the station moves from 11:40 p.m. to 1:00 a.m. until the paving is finished. All other routes keep their printed timetables, and the shelter displays at the plaza show live arrivals.','TTS','["Where passengers board from the library lane","The fare for the night route","The closure of the station","The route number of the shuttle"]','A',1,'Option A identifies the change: library lane riders now board at the north plaza.',87),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Transit notice. Starting today, the night shuttle replaces the library lane stops with a pick-up point at the north plaza because of roadwork, and the last departure from the station moves from 11:40 p.m. to 1:00 a.m. until the paving is finished. All other routes keep their printed timetables, and the shelter displays at the plaza show live arrivals.','TTS','["It leaves earlier","It leaves later than usual","It is cancelled","It runs twice"]','B',1,'Option B: the final trip shifts from 11:40 p.m. to 1:00 a.m., a later departure.',88),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Transit notice. Starting today, the night shuttle replaces the library lane stops with a pick-up point at the north plaza because of roadwork, and the last departure from the station moves from 11:40 p.m. to 1:00 a.m. until the paving is finished. All other routes keep their printed timetables, and the shelter displays at the plaza show live arrivals.','TTS','["Every route on campus will be rebuilt","The old library lane stop should return once the paving ends","The live displays will fail during the work","The night shuttle is being retired"]','B',1,'Option B infers from until the paving is finished that the change is temporary and the stop comes back afterwards.',89),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Let us finish the discussion of self-organisation with the best-studied example, the ant colony. A colony has no manager. Instead, workers respond to local cues, chiefly how often they bump into nestmates carrying a particular load. If foragers return slowly, encounters are rare, and idle workers drift toward the nest entrance to join the trail; if they speed up, meetings double, and ants abandon one task for another. Response thresholds differ among individuals, so some workers start a risky job sooner than their siblings, which keeps a reserve of specialists for sudden threats. The same feedback explains how colonies shift from brood care to foraging as they grow: older, larger nests simply hold ants who meet each other more. Notice what the model claims: a stable division of labour can arise from blind local rules with nobody planning it.','TTS','["Why ants fight over food","How a queen directs her workers","Which ants are the strongest","How simple local encounters produce an organised division of labour"]','D',1,'Option D states the lecture''s point: task allocation emerges from meeting rates, not from any planner.',90),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Let us finish the discussion of self-organisation with the best-studied example, the ant colony. A colony has no manager. Instead, workers respond to local cues, chiefly how often they bump into nestmates carrying a particular load. If foragers return slowly, encounters are rare, and idle workers drift toward the nest entrance to join the trail; if they speed up, meetings double, and ants abandon one task for another. Response thresholds differ among individuals, so some workers start a risky job sooner than their siblings, which keeps a reserve of specialists for sudden threats. The same feedback explains how colonies shift from brood care to foraging as they grow: older, larger nests simply hold ants who meet each other more. Notice what the model claims: a stable division of labour can arise from blind local rules with nobody planning it.','TTS','["How busy each task currently is","Which ants are the oldest","Where the nest will expand","How long the colony has existed"]','A',1,'Option A: encounter rates signal demand, because slow returns mean few meetings and vice versa.',91),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Let us finish the discussion of self-organisation with the best-studied example, the ant colony. A colony has no manager. Instead, workers respond to local cues, chiefly how often they bump into nestmates carrying a particular load. If foragers return slowly, encounters are rare, and idle workers drift toward the nest entrance to join the trail; if they speed up, meetings double, and ants abandon one task for another. Response thresholds differ among individuals, so some workers start a risky job sooner than their siblings, which keeps a reserve of specialists for sudden threats. The same feedback explains how colonies shift from brood care to foraging as they grow: older, larger nests simply hold ants who meet each other more. Notice what the model claims: a stable division of labour can arise from blind local rules with nobody planning it.','TTS','["They make some ants unable to work","They prepare the colony for unexpected changes","They stop foraging entirely","They grow only in small nests"]','B',1,'Option B uses the speaker''s point that bold individuals form a standing reserve for sudden threats.',92),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Let us finish the discussion of self-organisation with the best-studied example, the ant colony. A colony has no manager. Instead, workers respond to local cues, chiefly how often they bump into nestmates carrying a particular load. If foragers return slowly, encounters are rare, and idle workers drift toward the nest entrance to join the trail; if they speed up, meetings double, and ants abandon one task for another. Response thresholds differ among individuals, so some workers start a risky job sooner than their siblings, which keeps a reserve of specialists for sudden threats. The same feedback explains how colonies shift from brood care to foraging as they grow: older, larger nests simply hold ants who meet each other more. Notice what the model claims: a stable division of labour can arise from blind local rules with nobody planning it.','TTS','["Its workers meet less often, so fewer drift into foraging","Its queen does the foraging alone","It cannot care for brood","It collapses within a season"]','A',1,'Option A inverts the stated mechanism: small young nests mean fewer encounters, which keeps workers on in-nest tasks.',93),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Back to dating, because few methods changed archaeology like radiocarbon measurement. Living tissue absorbs carbon continuously, and the ratio of a rare isotope to common carbon stays fixed. At death the absorption stops and the rare isotope decays at a known rate, so the amount left acts as a clock reaching back roughly fifty thousand years. That clock has two well-known problems. Its tempo depends on the atmosphere the organism lived in, and tree rings showed that ancient air held more of the isotope than today''s does, so raw dates must be calibrated against rings of known age. Second, buried samples absorb later carbon, and careless excavation can make a bone seem centuries younger than it is. With pretreatment that strips contamination, and calibration curves built from rings and coral, radiocarbon turned relative pottery sequences into actual calendar years.','TTS','["To argue that pottery cannot be dated","To compare coral with timber","To explain how a physical method gave sites real years","To defend careless excavation"]','C',1,'Option C matches the lecture''s arc: the clock, its two problems, and the fixes that produced calendar dates.',94),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Back to dating, because few methods changed archaeology like radiocarbon measurement. Living tissue absorbs carbon continuously, and the ratio of a rare isotope to common carbon stays fixed. At death the absorption stops and the rare isotope decays at a known rate, so the amount left acts as a clock reaching back roughly fifty thousand years. That clock has two well-known problems. Its tempo depends on the atmosphere the organism lived in, and tree rings showed that ancient air held more of the isotope than today''s does, so raw dates must be calibrated against rings of known age. Second, buried samples absorb later carbon, and careless excavation can make a bone seem centuries younger than it is. With pretreatment that strips contamination, and calibration curves built from rings and coral, radiocarbon turned relative pottery sequences into actual calendar years.','TTS','["Decay speeds up inside bone","Calibration curves are unnecessary","The isotope vanishes below fifty thousand years","Past atmospheres differed from the modern one"]','D',1,'Option D is the first problem named: the clock''s tempo depends on the atmosphere at the time, hence calibration against rings.',95),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Back to dating, because few methods changed archaeology like radiocarbon measurement. Living tissue absorbs carbon continuously, and the ratio of a rare isotope to common carbon stays fixed. At death the absorption stops and the rare isotope decays at a known rate, so the amount left acts as a clock reaching back roughly fifty thousand years. That clock has two well-known problems. Its tempo depends on the atmosphere the organism lived in, and tree rings showed that ancient air held more of the isotope than today''s does, so raw dates must be calibrated against rings of known age. Second, buried samples absorb later carbon, and careless excavation can make a bone seem centuries younger than it is. With pretreatment that strips contamination, and calibration curves built from rings and coral, radiocarbon turned relative pottery sequences into actual calendar years.','TTS','["Heat destroys the isotope","Later carbon can make a sample look too young","Tree rings hide the original ratio","Fossils absorb water"]','B',1,'Option B is the second problem: samples take on younger carbon after burial, biasing dates downward.',96),
(@set04_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Professor: Back to dating, because few methods changed archaeology like radiocarbon measurement. Living tissue absorbs carbon continuously, and the ratio of a rare isotope to common carbon stays fixed. At death the absorption stops and the rare isotope decays at a known rate, so the amount left acts as a clock reaching back roughly fifty thousand years. That clock has two well-known problems. Its tempo depends on the atmosphere the organism lived in, and tree rings showed that ancient air held more of the isotope than today''s does, so raw dates must be calibrated against rings of known age. Second, buried samples absorb later carbon, and careless excavation can make a bone seem centuries younger than it is. With pretreatment that strips contamination, and calibration curves built from rings and coral, radiocarbon turned relative pottery sequences into actual calendar years.','TTS','["It could order finds relative to each other but seldom produced calendar years","It measured the ancient atmosphere directly","It needed no physical samples","It was less careful than modern digging"]','A',1,'Option A follows from the closing sentence: radiocarbon turned relative sequences into actual years, implying the older method was relative.',97);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["The","committee","will","review","every","proposal","before","Friday"]','the committee will review every proposal before friday',1,'English declarative order: subject (The committee) + verb phrase (will review) + object (every proposal) + time (before Friday).',98),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Students","must","reserve","their","lab","slots","before","noon"]','students must reserve their lab slots before noon',1,'Order: subject (Students) + modal verb (must reserve) + object (their lab slots) + deadline (before noon).',99),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["A","second","study","was","needed","to","confirm","the","hypothesis"]','a second study was needed to confirm the hypothesis',1,'Order: subject (A second study) + passive verb (was needed) + purpose clause (to confirm the hypothesis).',100),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Sound","waves","travel","more","slowly","through","water","than","air","does"]','sound waves travel more slowly through water than air does',1,'Comparison structure: subject (Sound waves) + verb with comparative adverb (travel more slowly) + through water + than-clause (air does).',101),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["The","museum","will","open","its","new","wing","in","the","spring"]','the museum will open its new wing in the spring',1,'Order: subject (The museum) + verb (will open) + object (its new wing) + time (in the spring).',102),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Few","plants","survive","because","the","soil","is","extremely","saline"]','few plants survive because the soil is extremely saline',1,'Order: main clause (Few plants survive) + because-clause with adjective phrase (the soil is extremely saline).',103),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Her","notes","were","so","detailed","that","the","team","kept","them"]','her notes were so detailed that the team kept them',1,'Result structure: subject (Her notes) + so + adjective (detailed) + that-clause (the team kept them).',104),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Everyone","agreed","the","report","needed","a","rewrite","before","publication"]','everyone agreed the report needed a rewrite before publication',1,'Order: subject (Everyone) + agreed + omitted-that clause (the report needed a rewrite) + time (before publication).',105),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to the registration office (about 50–80 words) explaining that an advising hold appeared on your record overnight and asking which document you must bring to clear it before the add-drop deadline on Friday. Greet the staff and sign your name.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. A strong answer states the problem, names the Friday deadline, asks one clear question, and keeps a polite register.',106),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to a visa officer you met at the study-abroad fair (about 50–80 words) thanking them for the table pre-evaluation and asking whether it counts toward next spring''s application and which records should be attached. Be polite and sign your name.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. A strong answer thanks the officer, asks both questions plainly, and lists the intended attachments.',107),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Professor post: Cities should require downtown buildings to use timber instead of concrete whenever safety rules allow it. Two students reply — Claire: "Timber stores carbon and a tower rises faster, so the rule is an easy win." — Diego: "Developers may simply pay more for the panels and pass the cost to renters; speed saves less than the material costs." State your own opinion and support it with a reason or example (about 80–100 words).',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. A strong answer takes a position, engages with at least one of the two replies, and adds a new reason or example.',108),
(@set04_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Professor post: Universities should make a second language optional rather than required for graduation. Two students reply — Mei: "Forced classes push students away from cultures they would explore on their own." — Owen: "A required course guarantees at least some exposure, and many students discover an interest they would have skipped." State your own opinion and support it with a reason or example (about 80–100 words).',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. A strong answer states a clear stance, responds to one of the students, and closes with a concrete example.',109);
